{"conjecture": "Software systems degrade not through the accumulation of errors, but through the gradual loss of design invariants—implicit constraints that once coordinated change across modules. As each locally correct modification is made without referencing these invariants, the system's behavior becomes an emergent property of historical accidents rather than intentional structure. The cost of change rises because developers must reverse-engineer the original design intent from its accumulated violations, a task with no local signal and no test coverage."}
